{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` and\n`src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` both declare `trace_id` as\n`[\"string\",\"null\"]`, described as \"Langfuse trace id when tracing is enabled; null otherwise\",\nbut `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` passes a hardcoded `None` at every site that emits it. Nothing in\nrebar produces a per-run identity, so a verdict cannot name the run that produced it and no\nheader can carry a run-stable value.\n\n## Approach\n\nMint one identity per gate run at the two boundaries that already resolve the run's config\nexactly once: `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:167` (plan-review, `ctx.ticket_id` in\nscope) and `:762` (completion-verifier, `ticket_id` in scope). Both open `gate_config(cfg)`,\nwhose contextvar every op reads back via `resolve_gate_config` (`config.py:99-107`). Attach it\nwith `dataclasses.replace(cfg, ...)` before `gate_config(cfg)`, so a caller-supplied config is\nnever mutated in place.\n\nThe identity carries exactly three values: `trace_id` (32 lowercase hex from\n`secrets.token_hex(16)`), `ticket_id` (the boundary's own), and `operation` (the literal\n`\"plan_review\"` or `\"completion_verifier\"`, per boundary). There is deliberately no `run_id`:\n`trace_id` already identifies the run and a second identifier would have no consumer.\n`ticket_id` and `operation` are minted here because the boundary is the only place they are\nknowable; their consumer is the delivery ticket's `${run:...}` vocabulary\n(`26ae-041f-dcc4-4279`, which depends on this one), and both are covered by criteria below so\nthey are verifiable in this change rather than resting on that follow-on.\n\nThe 32-hex lowercase shape matches the W3C trace-id format, so later work can supply a real\nOpenTelemetry trace id into the same field with no schema or format migration. Random per run\nrather than derived from the ticket id: a derived value would merge every re-review of a ticket\ninto one trace, and the verdict's own `trace_id` already provides the path back from a verdict\nto its run.\n\nThe value reaches pool workers unchanged because both plan-review pools submit through\n`generation._submit_ctx` — `ex.submit(contextvars.copy_context().run, fn, *args)`\n(`generation.py:154-165`), used by `pass1.py:426-427` and `container_stage.py:260`.\n\nPopulate the verdict by reading `req.config.trace_id` at **all three** `runner.py` sites that\ncurrently hardcode `None`:\n\n- `:254` — `FakeRunner.run`'s `mode == \"structured\"` branch, whose own comment reads \"Structured\n  mode (e.g. verify_completion)\". The completion verifier is structured-mode, so a fake-runner\n  completion verdict returns from here, not `:259`; omitting it would make the completion-path\n  criterion below unsatisfiable against the fake runner.\n- `:259` — `FakeRunner.run`'s findings branch.\n- `:719` — the real runner.\n\nThe two `FakeRunner` branches are selected by `req.mode`, so testing one says nothing about the\nother. The seam is `req.config`, not a runner attribute: the resolved gate config is handed to\nthe op as `RunRequest(config=...)` (`operations.py:209-212`), and `FakeRunner` holds no instance\nconfig, already reaching configuration only through `req.config` (`req.config.repo_path` at\n`:264`). Per-request derivations such as `max_output_cfg` build with `dataclasses.replace`, so\nthe identity rides through unchanged.\n\nBecause the emitted meaning of `trace_id` changes — from \"a Langfuse trace id, present only when\ntracing is enabled\" to \"a rebar-minted per-run correlation id, present inside any gate run\" —\nboth schema files that define that vocabulary are updated in the same change, so the documented\ncontract never contradicts the emitted value.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the identity fields on `LLMConfig`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — mint at both run boundaries.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — read `req.config.trace_id` at `:254`, `:259`, `:719`.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` — restate the `trace_id` description.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` — restate the `trace_id` description.\n- `tests/unit/test_run_correlation.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests plus schema validation. The mechanism is contextvar propagation and\nverdict field population, both of which run for real in-process; no subprocess or network\nboundary is involved.\n\n- Happy path: within one `gate_config` scope every `resolve_gate_config()` read returns the same\n  `trace_id`, and a produced verdict carries it.\n- Edge: two successive scopes differ; the value is `None` outside any gate run.\n- Concurrency: a value read inside a `_submit_ctx`-submitted pool task equals the submitting\n  thread's, exercising the real `copy_context()` seam rather than asserting on it structurally.\n- Real-runner path: `:719` is driven through `PydanticAIRunner.run` with the offline\n  `model_override` TestModel harness, so the real finalize path executes with no network call.\n  Every other verdict assertion is satisfiable through `FakeRunner`, which would leave the\n  `:719` edit unproven.\n- Both gate surfaces: the plan-review and completion-verifier boundaries each produce a verdict\n  carrying the run's `trace_id`, each validated against its own schema. Testing only the\n  plan-review side would leave half the changed surface unverified.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Inside one gate run `resolve_gate_config().trace_id` is identical across repeated reads;\n      checked by a test asserting equality across three reads in one scope.\n- [ ] Two gate runs produce different `trace_id` values; checked by a test over two scopes.\n- [ ] `trace_id` is 32 lowercase hex characters; checked by\n      `re.fullmatch(r\"[0-9a-f]{32}\", value)`.\n- [ ] A value read inside a `_submit_ctx` pool task equals the submitting thread's; checked by\n      a test submitting a reader through the real `_submit_ctx`.\n- [ ] Within a gate scope `resolve_gate_config().ticket_id` and `.operation` equal what the\n      boundary minted them from; checked by a test asserting both, for each boundary.\n- [ ] A plan-review verdict carries the run's `trace_id` and validates against\n      `review_result.schema.json`; checked by asserting the field and running the validator.\n- [ ] A completion-verifier verdict produced in a completion-verifier scope carries that run's\n      `trace_id` and validates against `completion_verdict.schema.json`; checked by asserting\n      the field equals `resolve_gate_config().trace_id` and running the validator.\n- [ ] Both schema files describe `trace_id` as a per-gate-run correlation id in W3C 32-hex\n      shape, present inside any gate run and null for standalone ops, with no residual Langfuse\n      claim; checked by a grep asserting \"Langfuse\" is absent from both descriptions.\n- [ ] The identity survives a per-request config derivation; checked by asserting\n      `max_output_cfg(cfg).trace_id == cfg.trace_id`.\n- [ ] A verdict produced through the real runner site `:719` carries `req.config.trace_id`;\n      checked by a test driving `PydanticAIRunner.run` inside a gate scope with the offline\n      `model_override` TestModel harness, so the assertion cannot be satisfied by a\n      `FakeRunner` branch.\n- [ ] Outside any gate run the identity is `None` and verdicts are unchanged; checked by a test\n      asserting `trace_id is None` for a standalone op."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787709557187372001,"uuid":"cac88b29-ce6d-4306-9bac-70e58e04f970"}